  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through next Monday)\nIssued at 1026 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nThe upper shortwave will continue to track northeastward into the \nHigh Plains through the first part of the extended period as the \nupper high centered across the ETX region amplifies slightly into \nportions of the lower MS River Valley. The FA will find itself \nsandwiched between the two systems, with southerly flow aloft \nprevailing across the region, which will continue to aid in \nbeneficial subtropical/monsoonal moisture transport into the region. \nAs a result, PWATs are expected to remain well above\nclimatological standards, ranging from 1.25\" to around 1.75\" \nacross portions of the area. As daily perturbations ripple \nthrough the flow aloft, we could see the potential for isolated to\nscattered storms each day from Wednesday through Saturday. The \nbest chance for precipitation looks to arrive Thursday and Friday \nas a secondary, more potent shortwave lifts northeast from the \nFour Corners into the High Plains. DESI LREF probabilities depict \na 70 to 90% chance for areas across the Caprock receiving at \nleast a half inch of precipitation, with lower probabilities \naround 30 to 60% for areas off the Caprock Wednesday through \nFriday. Meanwhile, NBM probabilities remain consistent with the \nDESI probs, with higher end amounts up to 3\" possible for areas \nacross the far southwestern Texas Panhandle. The overall severe \nthreat with this activity will be low, though forecast soundings \ndepicting long-skinny CAPE profiles with a well saturated column \nof moisture suggests that stronger storm cores could pose a risk \nof precipitation loading, which could lead to localized flooding \nand potentially strong to marginally severe wind gusts at times. \n\nAs far as temperatures go through the extended, we will see the \nfirst taste of fall across the Caprock regions, though temperatures \nwill actually be near normal. Which is hard to believe considering \nhow hot it has been over the last several weeks. Expect highs in the \nmid 70s to mid 90s Wednesday through Saturday, though we could \nsee cooler highs considering the expectation of lingering cloud \ncover most afternoons given the abundant moisture in place. Headed\ninto the weekend, we will begin to see a warming trend occur as \nthe upper ridge builds back over the region. However, there is \nindication from ensemble members on the potential for another cool\ndown by next Tuesday as our next disturbance develops and \nattempts to swing a cold front through the area.",
